---
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ChipList from "@/components/ChipList.astro";
import { SITE } from "@/site.config";

const languages = ["English", "French", "German", "Spanish", "Catalan"];
const interests = [
  "Knowledge Systems",
  "Information Architecture",
  "Applied AI",
  "Document Intelligence",
  "Machine Learning",
  "Open Source"
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description={`About ${SITE.fullName} and the purpose of didac-crst.com.`}
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="page-container about-page">
      <header class="page-hero">
        <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
        <h1 class="page-title">Engineering information that stays understandable.</h1>
        <div class="reading-flow">
          <p class="lede">
            I am an engineer working at the intersection of information systems, data,
            knowledge architecture, and applied AI.
          </p>
          <p>
            My focus is not on applying AI everywhere. I design systems by choosing the
            simplest reliable method for each problem: deterministic logic where rules are
            sufficient, statistical and machine-learning methods where they fit, and language
            models where semantic reasoning genuinely adds value.
          </p>
        </div>
      </header>

      <section class="about-section">
        <div class="section-label">
          <h2>How I work</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="section-body">
          <div class="reading-flow">
            <p>
              I approach problems as systems rather than isolated features.
            </p>
            <p>
              That usually means understanding where information comes from, how it changes,
              how reliable it is, how different concepts relate to one another, and which
              parts of the process should remain deterministic.
            </p>
            <p>
              I am particularly interested in architectures that combine structured data,
              documents, ontologies, classifiers, clustering, embeddings, and language models
              without making probabilistic AI responsible for problems that can be solved more
              predictably.
            </p>
          </div>
          <ol class="principle-flow" aria-label="Architectural principle">
            <li>Deterministic when possible</li>
            <li>Statistical when appropriate</li>
            <li>Semantic AI when valuable</li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="about-section">
        <div class="section-label">
          <h2>Experience</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="section-body">
          <ol class="timeline">
            <li>
              <div>
                <strong>Airbus</strong>
                <span>2011 → present</span>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <div>
                <strong>Information Systems</strong>
                <span>2011–2018</span>
              </div>
              <p>
                Started in information systems and project delivery, working on enterprise
                systems and the relationship between technical platforms and operational
                processes.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <div>
                <strong>Product Management</strong>
                <span>2018–2022</span>
              </div>
              <p>
                Moved into product management at Satair, adding product strategy, users,
                business priorities, and organizational constraints to the engineering
                perspective.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <div>
                <strong>Flight Data Analytics & Predictive Maintenance</strong>
                <span>2022–2025</span>
              </div>
              <p>
                Worked with aircraft operational data, analytics, and predictive-maintenance
                problems, strengthening the connection between data engineering, statistical
                analysis, and real-world decision making.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <div>
                <strong>Quality Engineering & Analytics</strong>
                <span>2026–present</span>
              </div>
              <p>
                Working across quality and engineering analytics, with a growing focus on how
                large organizations can turn fragmented operational information into structured
                and usable knowledge.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="about-section">
        <div class="section-label">
          <h2>One problem, different perspectives</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="section-body reading-flow">
          <p>
            Moving between information systems, product management, data analytics, and
            engineering quality has shaped how I approach architecture.
          </p>
          <p>
            Technical correctness is only one part of a useful system. It also needs to fit
            the organization, preserve the meaning of information, remain understandable over
            time, and support the people who ultimately make decisions with it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="about-section">
        <div class="section-label">
          <h2>Education</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="section-body">
          <p class="section-intro">
            My formal background combines engineering and business. Engineering gave me a
            systems perspective; the MBA added strategy, organizations, and economics to it.
          </p>
          <div class="education-list">
            <div class="education-item">
              <strong>ESADE</strong>
              <p>Executive MBA</p>
              <span>Exchange at International University of Japan</span>
            </div>
            <div class="education-item">
              <strong>INPG / UPC</strong>
              <p>MSc Electronic Engineering · Double degree</p>
            </div>
            <div class="education-item">
              <strong>UPC</strong>
              <p>MSc Telecommunications Engineering</p>
            </div>
            <div class="education-item">
              <strong>UPC</strong>
              <p>BSc Telecommunications Engineering</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="about-section compact">
        <div class="section-label">
          <h2>Languages</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="section-body">
          <ChipList items={languages} label="Languages" />
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="about-section compact">
        <div class="section-label">
          <h2>Current interests</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="section-body">
          <ChipList items={interests} label="Current interests" />
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="about-section">
        <div class="section-label">
          <h2>Building as a way of thinking</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="section-body">
          <div class="reading-flow">
            <p>
              Outside my professional work, I build systems to explore architectural ideas
              without the constraints of a large enterprise environment.
            </p>
          </div>
          <article class="feature-card">
            <p class="eyebrow">AtlasDocs</p>
            <h3>Document intelligence and knowledge architecture.</h3>
            <p>
              An open-source exploration of turning documents and metadata into structured,
              traceable knowledge while combining deterministic processing with AI only where
              it adds value.
            </p>
            <a class="button-link" href="/projects/atlasdocs/">View AtlasDocs</a>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="about-section compact">
        <div class="section-label">
          <h2>Writing</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="section-body reading-flow">
          <p>
            I use technical writing to make architectural decisions explicit: why a particular
            approach works, when it does not, and what trade-offs sit behind it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a class="button-link" href="/writing/">Explore writing</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about-page {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-8);
  }

  .reading-flow,
  .section-intro {
    max-width: var(--width-reading);
  }

  .reading-flow {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-4);
  }

  .reading-flow p,
  .section-intro,
  .timeline p,
  .education-list p,
  .feature-card p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-muted);
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.75;
  }

  .about-section {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(12rem, 0.32fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-6);
    padding-top: var(--space-7);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .about-section.compact {
    padding-top: var(--space-6);
  }

  .section-label h2 {
    position: sticky;
    top: 6rem;
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .section-body {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-5);
  }

  .principle-flow {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    max-width: 32rem;
    margin: var(--space-2) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .principle-flow li {
    position: relative;
    padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-5);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-medium);
    background: var(--color-surface);
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .principle-flow li:not(:last-child)::after {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: var(--space-5);
    bottom: -0.8rem;
    width: 1px;
    height: 0.8rem;
    background: var(--color-accent);
  }

  .timeline {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-5);
    max-width: 54rem;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .timeline li {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    padding-left: var(--space-5);
    border-left: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .timeline li > div {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--space-2);
  }

  .timeline strong {
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-size: 1.08rem;
  }

  .timeline span,
  .education-list span {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .education-list {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-5);
    max-width: var(--width-reading);
  }

  .education-item {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-1);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-4);
    border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-border) 72%, transparent);
  }

  .education-item:last-child {
    padding-bottom: 0;
    border-bottom: 0;
  }

  .education-item strong {
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-size: 1.12rem;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .feature-card {
    padding: var(--space-5);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-medium);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-surface) 86%, transparent);
  }

  .feature-card h3 {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
    font-size: 1.12rem;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .feature-card {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    max-width: 44rem;
  }

  .feature-card .eyebrow {
    margin: 0;
  }

  @media (max-width: 820px) {
    .about-section {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: var(--space-4);
    }

    .section-label h2 {
      position: static;
    }
  }
</style>
